  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 AM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nDry southwest flow will keep most of southern Colorado \nprecipitation free for Wednesday and Thursday with temperatures \nwarming back into the 90s across the plains and even some near \n100 degree readings across far eastern areas. Valleys will \nreturn into the 80s with 60s and 70s for the mountain areas. \nBreezy southwesterly afternoon winds may bring some spotty \ncritical fire weather conditions along portions of the I-25 \ncorridor and San Luis Valley where precipitation has been more \nscant. However these conditions do not look widespread enough to\nrequire any fire weather highlights. \n\nMonsoon moisture picks up again from the south and steadily \nincreases from Friday through the weekend.  This will bring a return \nof afternoon and evening thunderstorms with heavy rainfall possible. \nThe best chances for rain will be across the mountains and valleys \nwhere precipitable waters approach 140-160% of normal in \nensemble means. There will be rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms across the plains as well, but lee troughing and \nsouthwesterly surface flow keeps low level moisture quality more\nlimited. This should keep any heavier rainfall east of the \nmountains more spotty in coverage. \n\nNot much change into early next week with the moisture plume \noriented across western CO and daily afternoon and evening \nthunderstorm chances continuing.  Precipitable waters decrease a bit \nfor southern areas as the southern plains upper high expands \nwestward and nudges the best moisture plume across the Desert \nSouthwest and Great Basin towards the middle of next week. \nPrecipitable waters remain in the 110-140% of normal range which\nwill keep at least isolated to scattered thunderstorms in the \nforecast, the stronger of which could still produce locally \nheavy rainfall. Temperatures will remain above normal. -KT",
